/* Original Orthodox reading helps for OrthoBible.
   Structure follows a study-Bible pattern (book preamble + verse notes + how-to-read).
   Wording is not taken from The Orthodox Study Bible (copyright St. Athanasius Academy / Thomas Nelson).
   Typology and cross-references follow public-domain patristic and liturgical tradition.
   NOTE: if a local osb-notes.js file is present (personal use, not committed), it loads
   after this file and overrides STUDY.notes and STUDY.preambles with the owner's own
   Orthodox Study Bible annotations. */
window.STUDY = {
  about: "Book introductions, a short guide on how to read Scripture in the Church, and verse notes on typology and the Fathers. These are original notes, not the copyrighted Orthodox Study Bible apparatus.",

  essays: {
    "kallistos-reading": {
      title: "How to read Scripture",
      byline: "A summary of Metropolitan Kallistos Ware’s How to Read the Bible",
      lead: "Kallistos invites us to approach Scripture with attention, within the Church, with Christ at its center, and with a willingness to be changed.",
      personalGuideTitle: "How to Read the Bible",
      source: {
        url: "https://www.oca.org/reflections/misc-authors/how-to-read-the-bible",
        label: "Read the full guide on OCA",
      },
      sections: [
        { h: "Listen attentively", p: "Bring curiosity and openness. Notice the author, setting, and kind of writing, while remaining receptive to what God may be asking of you." },
        { h: "Read with the Church", p: "Explore how a passage is heard in worship and understood by the Fathers. Discuss difficult questions with your parish rather than carrying them alone." },
        { h: "Keep Christ at the center", p: "Look for connections across the Old and New Testaments and how they illuminate Christ’s life and work." },
        { h: "Make it personal", p: "After understanding the passage in context, consider your own life: where might it invite repentance, generosity, patience, or hope?" },
      ],
    },
    "how-to-read": {
      kicker: "A rule of reading",
      title: "How to Read the Bible",
      lead: "The Scriptures are not a private codebook. They are the Church’s book, read in the Church, toward Christ, for the healing of the soul.",
      sections: [
        {
          h: "Begin with prayer",
          p: "Open as the liturgy opens: “O Heavenly King, Comforter…” Ask the Spirit who spoke through the prophets to speak in you. A short prayer before a chapter does more than a long session without one. The Fathers treat reading as an act of standing before God, not as collecting facts."
        },
        {
          h: "The Bible belongs to the Church",
          p: "Christ did not leave a bound volume on a table. He left apostles, baptism, the breaking of the bread, and a people. The same Spirit who inspired the text abides in the Church that received it. That is why we read with the Fathers, the Councils, and the liturgical year, not against them. Private cleverness is not a charism."
        },
        {
          h: "Christ is the key",
          p: "On the road to Emmaus the risen Lord, “beginning at Moses and all the Prophets, expounded to them in all the Scriptures the things concerning Himself” (Luke 24:27). The Old Testament is not a closed Jewish preface that the New replaces. It is a single economy. If a reading of Genesis, Exodus, or the Psalms does not finally open onto the Incarnate Word, we have not yet read it as the Church reads it."
        },
        {
          h: "History first, then the figure",
          p: "The events happened. Abraham left Ur. Israel crossed the sea. David reigned. The Church does not dissolve history into allegory. But history is not a flat surface. The Fathers speak of several senses: the letter (what took place), the type (how it prefigures Christ and the Church), the moral sense (how it forms us), and the anagogical sense (how it points to the Kingdom). A good reader lets the letter stand, then asks what the Spirit is showing in Christ."
        },
        {
          h: "How the Old Testament prefigures Christ",
          p: "A type is a real person, event, or thing that God ordered so that it would later be recognized in its fulfillment. The ark is a type of the Church and of the Theotokos; the Red Sea, of baptism; the manna, of the Eucharist; the ram on Moriah, of the Lamb; Jonah’s three days, of the tomb; the bronze serpent, of the Cross. Types do not cancel the first meaning. They reveal why the first meaning was given."
        },
        {
          h: "Read with the Fathers, not instead of the text",
          p: "Chrysostom, Basil, Gregory the Theologian, Ephrem, Cyril of Alexandria, Maximus, and the rest are not a second Bible. They are the Church remembering how she has always heard this book. When they disagree in detail, keep the consensus: Trinity, Incarnation, the Church as Christ’s body, the call to holiness. Verse notes in this app mark that consensus, not a private system."
        },
        {
          h: "The liturgy is a commentary",
          p: "Holy Week, Pascha, Theophany, and the daily hours already interpret Scripture. The Church does not first write a footnote and then pray. She prays the verse, and the footnote is the prayer. If you want to know what Exodus 12 means, stand through a Paschal liturgy. If you want to know Psalm 50, say it at Matins until it is in the mouth."
        },
        {
          h: "The aim is communion, not mastery",
          p: "We read so that the mind may be purified and the heart become a dwelling of God. Information without ascesis puffs up. A chapter read slowly, kept through the day, and joined to almsgiving and the mysteries will take you further than a year of arguments. “You search the Scriptures,” the Lord says, “and these are they which testify of Me” (John 5:39)."
        },
        {
          h: "A simple daily pattern",
          p: "One psalm. One short Old Testament lesson, asking “Where is Christ here?” One Gospel paragraph. Close with the Trisagion or the Jesus Prayer. Mark what wounds you or consoles you. The bookmarks and highlights in this app are for that, not for display. If a day is hard, keep the psalm."
        }
      ]
    },
    typology: {
      kicker: "The Old in the New",
      title: "How the Scriptures Speak of Christ",
      lead: "The Church never reads the Old Testament as a museum of Israel’s past. She reads it as the first half of a single sentence whose verb is the Incarnation.",
      sections: [
        {
          h: "Promise, figure, fulfillment",
          p: "Some passages promise Christ in words (Isaiah 7:14; 9:6; 53; Micah 5:2; Psalm 21/22). Some show Him in figure (Isaac, Joseph, Moses, David, Jonah, the temple). The New Testament then names the fulfillment: “These things happened as types” (1 Corinthians 10:11). Hebrews is a long lesson in this method: priesthood, sacrifice, and sanctuary find their truth in the Cross and the heavenly liturgy."
        },
        {
          h: "Persons",
          p: "Adam is the type of Christ, the new Adam (Romans 5; 1 Corinthians 15). Eve, of the Church and, in another register, of the Theotokos. Abel’s blood cries; Christ’s blood speaks better things. Melchizedek, priest and king, bread and wine, is named in Hebrews as a figure of the Lord’s priesthood. Joseph, rejected by his brothers and saving them in famine, is read as a passion and exaltation of Christ. David the shepherd-king, Solomon the temple-builder, Jonah in the fish, and the three youths in the furnace are all taken this way in the hymns."
        },
        {
          h: "Events",
          p: "Creation by the Word; Paradise and the tree; the Flood and the ark; the binding of Isaac; the Exodus, Passover, manna, water from the rock, and the tabernacle; the entrance into the land; the exile and return. Each is a real act of God, and each is a sketch of baptism, Eucharist, the Cross, the Church, and the Resurrection. The liturgical year walks this path every Holy Week."
        },
        {
          h: "Things",
          p: "The burning bush that is not consumed: the Virgin bearing God. The mercy seat and the cherubim: the Incarnation as the true meeting-place. The bronze serpent lifted up: the Cross (John 3:14). The temple veil: His flesh (Hebrews 10:20). Oil, water, bread, wine, and light keep their Old Testament dignity and receive their end in the mysteries."
        },
        {
          h: "How to use the notes",
          p: "Gold marks on a verse mean a note is waiting under the chapter, in the old study-Bible manner. “Type” means a figure of Christ or the Church. “Trinity” marks a place the Fathers heard the three Persons. “Liturgy” ties a verse to a feast or hour. “See” jumps to the fulfillment or the figure. These notes are a start. The hymns of the Church will take you further than any footer."
        }
      ]
    }
  },

  preambles: {
    GEN: { author: "Moses, according to the Church’s tradition", date: "From the creation of the world to Joseph’s death in Egypt", theme: "Beginnings — of the cosmos, of humanity, of Israel, and of the promise", outline: "1–11 primeval history · 12–25 Abraham · 26–36 Isaac and Jacob · 37–50 Joseph", body: "Genesis is the seed of the whole Bible. God speaks, and there is light. The human being is made in the image, falls, and is not abandoned. The Fathers hear the Word in “Let there be light,” the Trinity in “Let Us make man,” the Cross in the ram of Moriah, the Theotokos in the unplundered Paradise of later hymnography, and Christ and the Church in the ark. Read it as the opening of one story that ends in a garden-tomb and an eighth day." },
    EXO: { author: "Moses", date: "The departure from Egypt and the covenant at Sinai", theme: "Deliverance, covenant, and the dwelling of God among His people", outline: "1–15 oppression and Passover · 16–18 wilderness · 19–24 Sinai · 25–40 tabernacle", body: "Exodus is the Church’s baptismal book. The sea, the cloud, the manna, the rock, and the Passover lamb are named as types in the New Testament itself. The tabernacle is the grammar of Orthodox worship: holy place, veil, bread of the Presence, lampstand. When you hear “I AM WHO I AM,” you are already on the ground of the Transfiguration and the Gospel of John." },
    LEV: { author: "Moses", date: "At Sinai, after the tabernacle is raised", theme: "Holiness: how a people lives with the Holy One in their midst", outline: "1–7 offerings · 8–10 priesthood · 11–15 purity · 16 Day of Atonement · 17–27 the holy people", body: "Leviticus is not a discarded manual. The sacrifices interpret the Cross; the Day of Atonement interprets Holy Friday; “Be holy, for I am holy” is quoted by Peter to the Church. The food laws and washings trained Israel to know that God is not common. Their end is the holiness of baptism and the chalice." },
    NUM: { author: "Moses", date: "The wilderness years", theme: "The pilgrim people, tested between promise and rest", outline: "1–10 census and departure · 11–25 rebellions · 21 bronze serpent · 22–24 Balaam · 26–36 plains of Moab", body: "Numbers is the book of the journey. The bronze serpent lifted up is named by Christ as the figure of His Cross (John 3:14). The water, the manna, and the failures of the people become Paul’s warning and consolation to the Corinthians. A parish is still a camp around the tabernacle." },
    DEU: { author: "Moses", date: "On the plains of Moab, at the edge of the land", theme: "Covenant renewed: hear, love, and remember", outline: "1–4 remembered history · 5–26 law of the heart · 27–30 blessings and curses · 31–34 Moses’ end", body: "Deuteronomy is Moses’ last homily. “Hear, O Israel” becomes the Lord’s first commandment. The prophet like Moses (18:15) is confessed as Christ in Acts. The book trains memory — the opposite of the wilderness of forgetting — and so it trains liturgy." },
    JOS: { author: "Joshua, in the tradition of Israel’s scribes", date: "The entrance into the land", theme: "Promise kept: the people cross and inherit", outline: "1–5 Jordan and Gilgal · 6–12 conquest · 13–22 allotment · 23–24 covenant at Shechem", body: "Joshua (Iesous in Greek) bears the Lord’s own name. The Jordan crossing is a second Exodus and a type of baptism. Rahab’s scarlet cord, the fall of Jericho, and the Captain of the Lord’s host are read in the hymns as the economy of salvation already at work." },
    JDG: { author: "Israel’s historians, in the spirit of the prophets", date: "The generations after Joshua", theme: "Without a king: sin, crying out, deliverance, relapse", outline: "1–2 prologue · 3–16 the judges · 17–21 “there was no king”", body: "Judges is a dark book on purpose. Each cycle is the soul’s cycle. Gideon, Barak, Samson, and the rest are named in Hebrews 11, not as moral mascots but as those who acted through faith. The cry for a true king is answered only in Christ." },
    RUT: { author: "Israel’s historians", date: "In the days of the judges", theme: "Covenant kindness, and a Gentile brought into the line of David", outline: "1 Moab to Bethlehem · 2 the field · 3 the threshing floor · 4 redemption and genealogy", body: "Ruth the Moabitess stands in the Lord’s genealogy (Matthew 1). Boaz the kinsman-redeemer is a figure of Christ, who takes a Gentile bride and restores the inheritance. Bethlehem already waits." },
    "1SA": { author: "Prophetic historians of Israel", date: "From Samuel to the rise of David", theme: "Kingship given, misused, and anointed toward David", outline: "1–7 Samuel · 8–15 Saul · 16–31 David and Saul", body: "Called 1 Kingdoms in the Orthodox order. Hannah’s song is the Magnificat in seed. David the shepherd-anointed, the harpist before the tormented king, and the one who will not seize the kingdom by murder, is the great type of the meek Christ." },
    "2SA": { author: "Prophetic historians of Israel", date: "David’s reign", theme: "The throne, the promise, and the wound in the house", outline: "1–10 the kingdom established · 11–20 sin and its fruit · 21–24 last words", body: "2 Kingdoms. Nathan’s prophecy (7) is the backbone of messianic hope: a son of David whose throne is forever. The Psalms grow out of this life. David’s repentance after Bathsheba is the Church’s own Psalm 50 (51)." },
    "1KI": { author: "Prophetic historians of Israel", date: "Solomon to the divided kingdom", theme: "Temple, wisdom, and the crack in the kingdom", outline: "1–11 Solomon · 12–16 division · 17–22 Elijah", body: "3 Kingdoms. Solomon’s temple and prayer dedicate the place God chooses; the true Temple is Christ’s body. Elijah, the prophet of Carmel and Horeb, stands with Moses at the Transfiguration and is the Forerunner’s figure." },
    "2KI": { author: "Prophetic historians of Israel", date: "To the exile of Israel and Judah", theme: "The end of the kingdoms, and the word that outlives them", outline: "1–13 Elisha · 14–17 fall of Samaria · 18–25 Hezekiah to exile", body: "4 Kingdoms. Elisha’s signs — water, oil, bread, a child raised — are read as prefiguring the Lord’s miracles. The exile is not the end of the covenant; it is the furnace in which the remnant is made." },
    "1CH": { author: "The Chronicler", date: "After the exile, looking back to David", theme: "Worship around the ark and the throne", outline: "1–9 genealogies · 10–29 David and the temple preparations", body: "1 Paraleipomenon. Chronicles retells history as liturgy: choirs, gatekeepers, the ark. The Church’s own memory is of this kind — not raw chronicle but worshipful remembrance." },
    "2CH": { author: "The Chronicler", date: "Solomon to the decree of Cyrus", theme: "Temple, repentance, restoration", outline: "1–9 Solomon · 10–36 kings of Judah to Cyrus", body: "The last word is not ruin but “Let him go up.” Return from exile is a type of Pascha and of the soul’s return. Hezekiah and Josiah show what royal repentance looks like." },
    EZR: { author: "Ezra the scribe, in the Chronicler’s tradition", date: "The return under Cyrus and the rebuilding", theme: "Restore the house, restore the people", outline: "1–6 the temple · 7–10 Ezra and the rebuilding of the people", body: "Called 1 Ezra in some Orthodox lists (with 1 Esdras as another book). The remnant, the rebuilt altar, and the reading of the Law are the grammar of every parish renewal." },
    NEH: { author: "Nehemiah", date: "The wall of Jerusalem rebuilt", theme: "A people set apart, a city made defensible for worship", outline: "1–7 the wall · 8–10 the Law read · 11–13 reform", body: "2 Ezra / Nehemiah. The wall is not xenophobia; it is the ascesis that lets a liturgy exist. Chapter 8 — the people standing for the reading — is a picture of the Church." },
    TOB: { author: "A devout Israelite of the diaspora, in the Church’s canon", date: "Among the exiles of the North", theme: "Almsgiving, marriage, and the angel of God on the road", outline: "1–3 Tobit’s suffering · 4–12 the journey with Raphael · 13–14 canticle and end", body: "Tobit is read for its mercy: bury the dead, give alms, honor marriage. Raphael (“God heals”) walks hidden, as Christ on the road. The fish, the gall, and the wedding are taken by some Fathers as figures of healing and of the Church as bride." },
    JDT: { author: "Israel’s storytellers, received by the Church", date: "Set in a time of imperial threat", theme: "God saves through a holy woman", outline: "1–7 the siege · 8–16 Judith and the victory", body: "Judith, like Deborah and like the Theotokos in a different key, shows that salvation is the Lord’s, and that purity and courage can cut the head from the tyrant. The Church has long heard in such women a type of the Virgin’s victory over the serpent." },
    ESG: { author: "The Greek Esther of the Seventy", date: "Among the Persians", theme: "Hidden providence; a queen for her people", outline: "Additions + the Hebrew narrative of risk and deliverance", body: "The LXX Esther names God where the Hebrew book is shy of the Name. Providence works in palaces as in deserts. A people marked for death is spared — a Passover in a foreign court." },
    EST: { author: "The Hebrew Esther, also in this file", date: "Among the Persians", theme: "Lots, fasting, and unexpected deliverance", outline: "1–2 the queen · 3–7 Haman · 8–10 Purim", body: "Read beside the Greek Esther. Fasting, petition, and the reversal of a death-decree are the Church’s own Paschal pattern in miniature." },
    "1MA": { author: "A historian of the Hasmonean struggle", date: "2nd century BC", theme: "Zeal for the Law and the cleansing of the temple", outline: "1–2 the persecution · 3–9 Judas · 9–16 Jonathan and Simon", body: "1 Maccabees remembers a people that would not become Greek in the heart. The rededication of the temple is the ancestor of every restoration of worship. Martyrdom here prepares the New Testament’s own martyrs." },
    "2MA": { author: "An epitomist of Jason of Cyrene", date: "The same crisis, told as theology", theme: "Resurrection, prayer for the dead, and the God who defends His house", outline: "1–2 letters · 3–15 the persecution and the temple", body: "2 Maccabees is why Orthodox Christians pray for the departed (12:39–45) and confess the resurrection of the body (7). The mother of the seven sons stands near the Theotokos in the Church’s memory of costly faith." },
    "3MA": { author: "A Hellenistic Jewish narrator, kept in the Orthodox canon", date: "Set under Ptolemy IV", theme: "God humbles a king who would violate the holy place", outline: "The threat to the Jews in Egypt and their deliverance", body: "3 Maccabees is a diaspora Passover: a tyrant, a people, a sudden turn. The holy place cannot be treated as a tourist’s court." },
    PSA: { author: "David, Asaph, the sons of Korah, Moses, and others; the Church’s hymnal", date: "Gathered across Israel’s life; 151 in the Greek Psalter", theme: "The whole of human life before God, prayed in Christ", outline: "1–41 · 42–72 · 73–89 · 90–106 · 107–150 (Greek numbering in the notes)", body: "The Psalms are the Lord’s own prayer book. He quotes them from the Cross. The Church reads many in two registers: David’s history, and Christ’s voice (or the Church’s voice in Him). Psalm 50 (51) is the daily repentance of the hours; 90 (91) the prayer of protection; 103 (104) vespers; 148–150 the lauds of the world." },
    PS2: { author: "The Greek Psalter’s additional psalm", date: "Attached to the Psalter of the Seventy", theme: "David the shepherd, taken from the flocks", outline: "A single psalm", body: "Psalm 151 remembers the youngest son chosen over his brothers. The Church hears the meek King." },
    JOB: { author: "An ancient sage; the Church reads Job as a righteous sufferer", date: "Set in a patriarchal world", theme: "Innocent suffering, the limit of friends’ theology, the Lord who answers", outline: "1–2 prologue · 3–37 the debate · 38–42 the Lord speaks", body: "Job is a type of Christ the innocent sufferer, and of every believer in the furnace. The “friends” are what happens when doctrine is used to protect the speaker. The Lord does not explain the wound; He appears." },
    PRO: { author: "Solomon and other wise men of Israel", date: "Royal wisdom, gathered for the young", theme: "The fear of the Lord, and Wisdom who calls in the streets", outline: "1–9 Wisdom’s speeches · 10–29 sentences · 30–31 words and the valiant woman", body: "Wisdom in Proverbs 8 is read by the Fathers as the Word, the Son, through whom the Father made the ages. The valiant woman of 31 is read both morally and as a figure of the Church." },
    ECC: { author: "Qoheleth, in Solomon’s persona", date: "A royal looking-back", theme: "Vanity of what passes; fear God anyway", outline: "1–6 vanity under the sun · 7–12 wisdom in the face of death", body: "Ecclesiastes keeps us from baptizing success. The empty circle “under the sun” is what life is without the eighth day. The book’s last word — fear God and keep the commandments — is already turning toward Christ." },
    SNG: { author: "Solomon, in the Church’s traditional ascription", date: "A wedding song of Israel", theme: "The love of the Bridegroom and the Bride", outline: "The courtship, wound, search, and seal of love", body: "The Song is not embarrassed of the body, and it is not only about the body. The Fathers, from Hippolytus and Gregory of Nyssa onward, read it as Christ and the Church, or the Word and the soul. Holy Week and Pascha are full of its images." },
    WIS: { author: "A sage of the Greek-speaking Church’s Old Testament", date: "Late Second Temple, in the stream of Solomon’s wisdom", theme: "Wisdom as the radiance of God; the righteous one persecuted", outline: "1–5 the righteous · 6–9 Solomon’s prayer · 10–19 Wisdom in Israel’s history", body: "Wisdom 2 is among the clearest Old Testament passion prophecies: the righteous man condemned because his life accuses the ungodly. Chapter 7’s praise of Wisdom is heard of the Son and of the Spirit. This book is why the Orthodox canon is not the Protestant one." },
    SIR: { author: "Jesus son of Sirach", date: "Early 2nd century BC, Jerusalem, then Egypt", theme: "The fear of the Lord in work, speech, family, and worship", outline: "1–23 wisdom in ordinary life · 24 Wisdom’s hymn · 25–43 maxims · 44–50 the fathers · 51 prayer", body: "Sirach (Ecclesiasticus) is the Church’s old catechism of manners. Wisdom’s hymn in 24, taking rest in Zion, is read of the Word’s dwelling and of the Theotokos. “Let us now praise famous men” is the ancestor of every synaxarion." },
    HOS: { author: "Hosea of Israel", date: "8th century BC, as the North collapsed", theme: "God’s marriage to an unfaithful people", outline: "1–3 the prophet’s marriage · 4–14 the lawsuit and the plea", body: "Hosea’s marriage is the Lord’s own heart. “Out of Egypt I called My son” is taken up in Matthew 2 of Christ. “I desire mercy and not sacrifice” is on the Lord’s own lips. The wounded Husband is the Gospel in the prophets." },
    AMO: { author: "Amos of Tekoa", date: "8th century BC", theme: "Justice in the gate; the Day of the Lord is not a picnic", outline: "1–2 oracles on the nations · 3–6 Israel accused · 7–9 visions and restoration", body: "Amos will not let liturgy cover theft. The true feast is righteousness. The booth of David raised up (9:11) is heard of Christ’s kingdom in Acts 15." },
    MIC: { author: "Micah of Moresheth", date: "8th century BC, Judah", theme: "Judgment, a ruler from Bethlehem, walking humbly", outline: "1–3 judgment · 4–5 hope · 6–7 the Lord’s requirement", body: "Micah 5:2 is read at Nativity: the ruler from Bethlehem whose goings-forth are from of old. Chapter 6:8 is the moral sense in a single line — and still not the whole Gospel, which is that God Himself has come down." },
    JOL: { author: "Joel", date: "A day of locusts and of the Spirit", theme: "Rend your hearts; I will pour out My Spirit", outline: "1–2 the locust plague and repentance · 2–3 the Spirit and the nations", body: "Peter’s Pentecost sermon is a reading of Joel. The locusts are both insect and army and the soul’s wasting. The remedy is a fast that becomes a feast." },
    OBA: { author: "Obadiah", date: "After a wound from Edom", theme: "Pride of the high places, and the kingdom becoming the Lord’s", outline: "A single oracle against Edom and for Zion", body: "The shortest prophet. Edom is Esau, the brother who watched Judah fall. The last word is liturgical: the kingdom shall be the Lord’s." },
    JON: { author: "Jonah, in the prophetic tradition", date: "Set in the Assyrian world", theme: "The reluctant prophet, the sea, the city that repents", outline: "1 the flight · 2 the fish · 3 Nineveh · 4 the plant", body: "Christ names Jonah as the sign of His three days in the heart of the earth (Matthew 12:40). The Church paints him in the belly as a tomb-icon. Nineveh’s repentance shames the insider who loves his shade more than souls." },
    NAM: { author: "Nahum of Elkosh", date: "The fall of Nineveh", theme: "Woe to the city of blood", outline: "The vision of Nineveh’s end", body: "Nahum is the other side of Jonah. Mercy was offered; cruelty returned. The Lord is slow to anger and great in power, and He will not clear the guilty forever." },
    HAB: { author: "Habakkuk", date: "On the eve of Babylon", theme: "How long? The just shall live by faith", outline: "1 the complaint · 2 the watch · 3 the psalm", body: "Habakkuk’s question is Job’s in national form. The answer is not a theory but a vision: the earth filled with the knowledge of the glory of the Lord. Chapter 3 is already a canticle." },
    ZEP: { author: "Zephaniah", date: "In Josiah’s generation", theme: "The Day of the Lord, and a remnant that sings", outline: "1 the Day · 2 the nations · 3 the remnant’s joy", body: "“The Lord your God in your midst, the Mighty One, will save.” Zephaniah ends in a wedding-song. Holy Week knows this turn from wrath to dancing." },
    HAG: { author: "Haggai", date: "520 BC, the second year of Darius", theme: "Build the house", outline: "Four dated oracles on the temple", body: "Haggai will not let the returned exiles panel their own houses while the Lord’s lies waste. The latter glory of the house is heard of the Church and of the Lord’s coming to the temple." },
    ZEC: { author: "Zechariah", date: "With Haggai, after the exile", theme: "Visions of a cleansed city; the pierced King", outline: "1–8 night visions · 9–14 the shepherd-king", body: "Zechariah is a Holy Week prophet: the King humble and riding on a donkey, thirty pieces of silver, the Shepherd struck, “they shall look on Me whom they pierced.” The lampstand and the two olive trees feed Revelation as well." },
    MAL: { author: "Malachi (“My messenger”)", date: "After the temple is up, when worship has gone cold", theme: "True priesthood, and the sun of righteousness", outline: "1–2 priests and marriage · 3–4 the messenger and the Day", body: "Malachi names the Forerunner and, in the Church’s hearing, the Eucharist: “from the rising of the sun… a pure offering.” The sun of righteousness with healing in His wings is a Nativity and Pascha word." },
    ISA: { author: "Isaiah of Jerusalem, and the Isaian school the Church reads as one book", date: "8th century through the hope of return", theme: "The Holy One of Israel; Emmanuel; the Suffering Servant", outline: "1–39 the Assyrian crisis · 40–55 consolation and the Servant · 56–66 the new Zion", body: "Isaiah is the fifth Gospel to the Fathers. Virgin birth (7:14), the child named Mighty God (9:6), the Branch, the Servant who is wounded for our transgressions (53), the Spirit upon Him (61) — this is already the iconostasis of Christ. The trisagion of chapter 6 is our liturgy." },
    JER: { author: "Jeremiah of Anathoth", date: "The last decades of Judah", theme: "The word as fire; a new covenant written on the heart", outline: "1–25 judgment · 26–45 the prophet’s life · 46–51 the nations · 52 the fall", body: "Jeremiah weeps as Christ weeps over Jerusalem. The new covenant (31) is quoted in Hebrews as the charter of the Church. His life is itself a passion: cistern, stocks, and a word that will not stay shut." },
    BAR: { author: "Baruch, Jeremiah’s scribe, in the Greek canon", date: "In the exile’s shadow", theme: "Confession, wisdom, and hope for Jerusalem", outline: "1–3 confession · 3–4 wisdom · 4–5 consolation · Letter of Jeremiah (ch. 6)", body: "Baruch is the exile’s prayer-book. Wisdom appearing on earth and living among men is heard of the Incarnation. The Letter of Jeremiah (often Baruch 6) mocks idols so that the heart may travel light." },
    LAM: { author: "Jeremiah, in the Church’s ascription", date: "After 586 BC", theme: "How a city sits solitary, and still says “Great is Thy faithfulness”", outline: "Five dirges", body: "Lamentations is Holy Saturday’s tone. The Church does not skip the ruin on the way to the angel. “The Lord is my portion” is spoken from the rubble." },
    EZK: { author: "Ezekiel the priest, among the exiles", date: "6th century BC, by the Chebar", theme: "The glory departs and returns; dry bones live", outline: "1–24 judgment · 25–32 the nations · 33–39 restoration · 40–48 the new temple", body: "Ezekiel’s visions feed the Church’s iconography: the four living creatures, the dry bones of Pascha, the closed east gate read of the Virgin, the river from the temple read of baptism and of the Spirit. A priest in exile learns that glory is not trapped in a building." },
    DAN: { author: "Daniel, with the Church’s Greek additions", date: "The exile and the visions of the kingdoms", theme: "Faithfulness in the furnace; the Son of Man", outline: "1–6 the court tales · 7–12 the visions · Greek: Song of the Three, Susanna, Bel", body: "The three youths in the furnace — and a fourth like a son of God — are a Paschal and Nativity icon. The Son of Man coming with the clouds is the Lord’s own title. The Greek additions (in DAG in this app) are sung at Matins of Holy Saturday." },
    DAG: { author: "The Seventy’s Daniel", date: "The Greek book as the Church reads it", theme: "The same Daniel, with the hymn in the fire and the tales of Susanna and Bel", outline: "The additions around the Hebrew chapters", body: "Use this beside Daniel. The Song of the Three is the Church’s cosmic lauds: bless the Lord, all works of the Lord." },
    "1ES": { author: "The Greek 1 Esdras", date: "Overlapping Ezra–Nehemiah–2 Chronicles", theme: "Restoration, and the contest of what is strongest (truth)", outline: "Josiah to the reading of the Law, with the tale of the three guards", body: "1 Esdras is kept in the Orthodox appendix. Truth wins the contest in Darius’s court — a wisdom scene on the way back to the temple." },
    "2ES": { author: "The Ezra apocalypse (2 Esdras / 4 Ezra in Latin numbering)", date: "After the second temple’s grief, in apocalyptic voice", theme: "Why Zion fell, and the vision of the Man from the sea", outline: "The dialogues with Uriel and the visions", body: "A hard, luminous book. The Man from the sea is heard of the Messiah. It sits in the appendix because the Church is cautious with apocalypses, not because she is afraid of hope." },
    MAN: { author: "Manasseh’s prayer, as the Church received it", date: "A penitential psalm in a king’s name", theme: "No sin is beyond mercy", outline: "A single prayer", body: "The Prayer of Manasseh is Great Compline material in spirit: I have sinned above the number of the sand, and I bend the knee of my heart. Keep it next to Psalm 50." },
    "4MA": { author: "A philosophical homily on the Maccabean martyrs", date: "Late Second Temple", theme: "Reasoned piety masters the passions", outline: "The martyrdoms retold as a lesson in courage", body: "4 Maccabees is in the appendix. It reads the martyrs through the language of the virtues. The Church keeps the martyrs; she does not need the Stoic frame, but she honors the courage." },
    MAT: { author: "Matthew, one of the Twelve", date: "A Gospel for a Church of Jews and Gentiles", theme: "Jesus the Messiah, the new Moses, God with us", outline: "1–2 infancy · 3–7 kingdom preached · 8–10 deeds · 11–13 controversy and parables · 14–20 the road · 21–28 Passion and Resurrection", body: "Matthew opens with a genealogy and Emmanuel. Five teaching blocks answer the Torah. The Sermon on the Mount is the new Sinai. Read it to see how the Old Testament is not discarded but filled." },
    MRK: { author: "Mark, Peter’s interpreter", date: "The shortest Gospel, urgent, for a persecuted Church", theme: "The Son of God, hidden in power and revealed on the Cross", outline: "1–8 Galilee · 8–10 the way of the Cross · 11–16 Jerusalem", body: "Mark begins at the Jordan and moves like a man running. The first confession is a demon’s; the first human confession is Peter’s; the first complete confession is a centurion’s at the Cross. That is the pedagogy." },
    LUK: { author: "Luke the physician, companion of Paul", date: "A Gospel of the poor, of women, of the Spirit, written to Theophilus", theme: "The Savior of the world, from the temple to Emmaus", outline: "1–2 infancy canticles · 3–9 Galilee · 9–19 the journey · 19–24 Passion, table, Emmaus", body: "Luke gives us the Theotokos’s own voice (Magnificat), Zacharias, Symeon, and Anna. The lost sheep, the prodigal, Emmaus: recognition in the breaking of the bread. Acts is volume two." },
    JHN: { author: "John the Theologian, the beloved disciple", date: "The last Gospel, from the heart of the Incarnation", theme: "The Word became flesh; signs that you may believe", outline: "1 prologue · 2–12 signs · 13–17 the upper room · 18–21 Passion and breakfast on the shore", body: "John is the eagle. The prologue is Genesis rewritten in the light of the Face. Water, wine, bread, light, the shepherd, the vine — the mysteries are all here. Holy Week cannot be served without this book." },
    ACT: { author: "Luke", date: "From the Ascension to Paul’s Roman house-arrest", theme: "The Spirit and the Church to the ends of the earth", outline: "1–7 Jerusalem · 8–12 Judea and Samaria · 13–28 the nations", body: "Acts is the Gospel of the Holy Spirit. Pentecost reverses Babel. The Church’s orders — bishop, presbyter, deacon — appear in seed. Read it to remember that mission is not a program but a descent of fire." },
    ROM: { author: "Paul", date: "From Corinth, toward a visit to Rome", theme: "The righteousness of God, for Jew and Gentile, in Christ", outline: "1–4 the human plight and Abraham · 5–8 Adam, baptism, the Spirit · 9–11 Israel · 12–16 the living sacrifice", body: "Romans is the architecture of salvation: Adam and Christ, baptism into death, no condemnation, the grafting of the nations. It is not a license to skip the Church; it is the Church’s own account of mercy." },
    "1CO": { author: "Paul", date: "To a gifted, divided parish", theme: "The Cross vs. party spirit; the body; the resurrection", outline: "1–4 the Cross · 5–6 holiness · 7–14 body life and worship · 15 resurrection", body: "If your parish is gifted and messy, this is your book. The Eucharist is already tradition received (11). Love in chapter 13 is not a wedding poem first; it is how a body with many charisms does not tear." },
    "2CO": { author: "Paul", date: "After pain between apostle and parish", theme: "Ministry as dying and rising; strength in weakness", outline: "1–7 reconciliation · 8–9 the collection · 10–13 the fool’s speech", body: "The most personal Paul. Treasure in clay jars; the thorn; my grace is sufficient. Apostleship looks like the Cross, not like a brand." },
    GAL: { author: "Paul", date: "To Churches of Galatia", theme: "The Gospel is not the Law plus Christ", outline: "1–2 biography of a calling · 3–4 Abraham and the heir · 5–6 freedom and the Spirit", body: "Galatians protects the Cross from being made a footnote to ethnic boundary-markers. Freedom is not the passions; it is the Spirit’s fruit." },
    EPH: { author: "Paul", date: "From imprisonment", theme: "The Church as Christ’s body and bride, chosen before the ages", outline: "1–3 the mystery · 4–6 the worthy walk, household, armor", body: "Ephesians is the Church’s looking-glass: one body, one Spirit, one baptism. Marriage is an icon of Christ and the Church. The armor of chapter 6 is baptismal." },
    PHP: { author: "Paul", date: "From imprisonment, to a beloved Church", theme: "Joy, and the mind of Christ who emptied Himself", outline: "1 chains and Gospel · 2 the hymn · 3 the prize · 4 peace", body: "The hymn of 2:5–11 is already creed: He emptied Himself, even to death on a cross, and is given the Name. Kenosis is not an ethic bolted on; it is God." },
    COL: { author: "Paul", date: "From imprisonment, against a shrinking of Christ", theme: "The Son is the image of the invisible God; you are complete in Him", outline: "1–2 the cosmic Christ · 3–4 the hidden life", body: "Colossians will not let angels, diets, or visions outrank the Head. Baptism is burial and rising. The old human is stripped; the new is put on." },
    "1TH": { author: "Paul", date: "Among his earliest letters", theme: "A young Church, holy waiting, the coming of the Lord", outline: "1–3 thanksgiving and visit · 4–5 holiness and the Day", body: "Work, watch, pray. The dead in Christ rise first. Hope is not a mood; it is a Person coming." },
    "2TH": { author: "Paul", date: "A second word on the Day", theme: "Do not be quickly shaken; stand", outline: "1 encouragement · 2 the man of lawlessness · 3 work", body: "Eschatology without hysteria. The mystery of lawlessness is already at work; the Lord will end it. Keep the tradition." },
    "1TI": { author: "Paul to Timothy", date: "To a young bishop-apostolic delegate in Ephesus", theme: "Order, sound teaching, godliness", outline: "1 the charge · 2–3 prayer and orders · 4–6 the good minister", body: "The Pastoral Epistles are how the Church understood herself as a household of God: bishop, deacons, widows, the mystery of godliness — God was manifested in the flesh." },
    "2TI": { author: "Paul to Timothy", date: "A last letter, from chains", theme: "Fan the gift; all Scripture; come before winter", outline: "1 not ashamed · 2 a good soldier · 3–4 the deposit", body: "The Bible of the Church is here confessed as God-breathed and useful. The tone is a will and a flame: keep the pattern of sound words." },
    TIT: { author: "Paul to Titus in Crete", date: "Ordering young Churches", theme: "Elders in every city; grace that trains us", outline: "1 appointments · 2 the household of grace · 3 the washing of regeneration", body: "Titus 3 is a baptismal creed in miniature: the kindness of God our Savior, the washing of regeneration and renewing of the Holy Spirit." },
    PHM: { author: "Paul to Philemon", date: "A runaway, a master, a new brotherhood", theme: "Onesimus received no longer as a slave but as a brother", outline: "A single letter", body: "The Gospel does not write a treatise on slavery here; it places a man in the Lord’s own body and lets that fact do the work. A parish is tested by whom it will receive." },
    HEB: { author: "A teacher of the apostolic Church (the East did not insist on a name)", date: "To believers tempted to shrink back from the new covenant", theme: "Christ the true priest, sacrifice, and sanctuary", outline: "1–4 the Son above angels and Moses · 5–10 the priesthood · 11–13 faith and the city to come", body: "Hebrews is the Church’s master-class in typology. Melchizedek, the veil, the blood, the once-for-all offering — the Old is not thrown away; it is opened. This is how to read Leviticus after Pascha." },
    JAS: { author: "James, the Lord’s brother, first bishop of Jerusalem", date: "To the twelve tribes in the dispersion", theme: "Faith that works; the tongue; the poor", outline: "Trials, hearing and doing, the royal law, patience", body: "James will not let Paul be turned into a slogan. The royal law is mercy. Anointing the sick (5) is still the Church’s mystery of holy unction." },
    "1PE": { author: "Peter", date: "To the elect strangers of Asia Minor", theme: "A holy nation, sharing Christ’s sufferings", outline: "1–2 identity · 2–3 household · 3–5 suffering and shepherds", body: "Baptism saves as an antitype of the ark (3:21). The Church is a priesthood. Elders shepherd; the chief Shepherd appears. Exile is the Christian address." },
    "2PE": { author: "Peter", date: "A farewell against false teachers", theme: "Partakers of the divine nature; the Day of the Lord", outline: "1 the virtues · 2 false teachers · 3 the new heavens", body: "Theosis is named here without embarrassment: partakers of the divine nature. Scripture is not of private interpretation. The world is not a machine winding down; it waits to be transfigured." },
    "1JN": { author: "John", date: "Against those who deny that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh", theme: "God is light, God is love; confess the Incarnation", outline: "Walk in light · love the brethren · test the spirits · perfect love", body: "The Incarnation is the test of every spirit. You cannot love God whom you have not seen if you will not love the brother you have. Confession, not self-invention, is the path." },
    "2JN": { author: "John the elder", date: "To a chosen lady and her children", theme: "Walk in love; do not receive the deceiver", outline: "A short letter of truth and boundaries", body: "Hospitality is a virtue; hospitality to the denial of the flesh of Christ is not. The lady is often read as a Church." },
    "3JN": { author: "John the elder", date: "To Gaius", theme: "Receive the brethren; Diotrephes loves preeminence", outline: "A short letter of names", body: "Parish politics are already here. Imitate not what is evil but what is good. Missionaries need tables, not only sermons." },
    JUD: { author: "Jude, the Lord’s brother", date: "Against those who turn grace into license", theme: "Contend for the faith once delivered", outline: "A single urgent letter", body: "Jude quotes Enoch and disputes about Moses’ body because the tradition is wider than a slim canon of proof-texts. Keep yourselves in the love of God." },
    REV: { author: "John the Theologian", date: "From Patmos, to seven Churches of Asia", theme: "The Lamb who was slain is on the throne; worship and witness until He comes", outline: "1–3 the Churches · 4–11 the Lamb and the seals · 12–20 war and judgment · 21–22 the city", body: "Revelation is liturgy before it is a puzzle. The hymns, the altar, the incense, the marriage supper — this is already Divine Liturgy seen from the inside of heaven. Read it with the Fathers, not with newspaper timelines. The City needs no temple, for the Lord and the Lamb are its temple." }
  },

  notes: {
    "GEN:1:1": { kind: "type", title: "In the beginning", body: "John opens with the same words. The Word through whom all things were made is the Son. Creation is already a revelation of Christ, not a closed physical prologue.", see: ["JHN:1:1", "COL:1:16"] },
    "GEN:1:3": { kind: "type", title: "Let there be light", body: "The first spoken word of God is light. The Fathers hear the Word, and Pascha: “Christ is risen from the dead, trampling down death by death.” Baptism is illumination.", see: ["JHN:1:4", "2CO:4:6"] },
    "GEN:1:26": { kind: "trinity", title: "Let Us make man", body: "The plural is not a royal flourish to the Fathers. It is the first glimmer of the three Persons. The image is fulfilled when the Image Himself takes our clay.", see: ["COL:1:15", "JHN:14:9"] },
    "GEN:2:7": { kind: "type", title: "Breath of life", body: "Adam becomes a living soul by the breath of God. The last Adam is a life-giving Spirit (1 Cor 15:45). Pentecost is this verse given to the Church.", see: ["JHN:20:22", "1CO:15:45"] },
    "GEN:2:21": { kind: "type", title: "The sleep of Adam", body: "From the side of the sleeping Adam, the woman. From the side of the sleeping Christ on the Cross, blood and water — Eucharist and baptism, the Church.", see: ["JHN:19:34", "EPH:5:31"] },
    "GEN:3:6": { kind: "type", title: "The tree", body: "By a tree the fall; by a tree the raising up. The Cross is the tree of life restored. Holy Friday’s hymns will not let you miss this.", see: ["GAL:3:13", "REV:22:2"] },
    "GEN:3:15": { kind: "type", title: "The seed of the woman", body: "The first Gospel: enmity between the serpent and the woman, her seed bruising his head. Read of Christ, and of the Theotokos who bore Him.", see: ["ISA:7:14", "LUK:1:31"] },
    "GEN:3:21": { kind: "type", title: "Garments of skin", body: "God clothes the fallen pair. The Fathers see both mercy and mortality. Baptism will strip the old coats and put on Christ.", see: ["GAL:3:27"] },
    "GEN:6:14": { kind: "type", title: "The ark", body: "One ark, through water, to a new world. Peter names baptism the antitype (1 Pet 3:20–21). The ark is also read of the Church, and of the Theotokos who bore the New Creation.", see: ["1PE:3:20", "MAT:24:38"] },
    "GEN:12:1": { kind: "type", title: "Go from your land", body: "Abraham’s exodus is the beginning of the people. Faith is a departure. The same voice will say, “Follow Me.”", see: ["HEB:11:8", "MAT:4:19"] },
    "GEN:14:18": { kind: "type", title: "Melchizedek", body: "King of Salem, priest of God Most High, bread and wine — named in Psalm 109 (110) and opened in Hebrews as the figure of Christ’s priesthood, not from Levi.", see: ["PSA:110:4", "HEB:7:1"] },
    "GEN:18:1": { kind: "trinity", title: "The three visitors", body: "Abraham’s hospitality at Mamre: three, and he speaks as to one. The Church’s icon of the Trinity begins here. The table is already eucharistic in the hymns.", see: ["JHN:8:56", "HEB:13:2"] },
    "GEN:22:2": { kind: "type", title: "The binding of Isaac", body: "The beloved son carries the wood up the mountain. The ram is given in his place. The Fathers will not let you read this without Golgotha. “God will provide the lamb.”", see: ["JHN:1:29", "HEB:11:17"] },
    "GEN:28:12": { kind: "type", title: "Jacob’s ladder", body: "Angels ascending and descending. Christ tells Nathanael that this is about the Son of Man (John 1:51). The Theotokos is also named “ladder” in the Akathist: God comes down by her.", see: ["JHN:1:51"] },
    "GEN:32:28": { kind: "type", title: "Israel — he who wrestles with God", body: "Jacob’s new name is the Church’s. We do not skip the wound. The face of God is asked for, and the limp remains.", see: ["HOS:12:4"] },
    "GEN:37:28": { kind: "type", title: "Joseph sold", body: "The beloved son, coat stripped, sold for silver, numbered with slaves, then saving the brothers who rejected him. Holy Week reads Joseph as a passion-icon of Christ.", see: ["MAT:26:15", "ACT:7:9"] },
    "GEN:49:10": { kind: "type", title: "Until Shiloh come", body: "The scepter from Judah. The Church hears the King from David’s tribe, whose is the obedience of the peoples.", see: ["MAT:1:1", "REV:5:5"] },
    "EXO:3:2": { kind: "type", title: "The bush not consumed", body: "Fire in the bush, the bush remains. The Fathers see the Unburnt Bush as the Virgin bearing God without corruption, and as the mystery of the Incarnation itself.", see: ["LUK:1:35", "EXO:3:14"] },
    "EXO:3:14": { kind: "trinity", title: "I AM", body: "The Name. In John, Jesus takes it on His own lips (“before Abraham was, I AM”). The burning ground is Gethsemane and Tabor as well as Horeb.", see: ["JHN:8:58"] },
    "EXO:12:3": { kind: "type", title: "The Passover lamb", body: "A lamb without blemish, blood on the door, a meal in haste, a departure from slavery. Paul: “Christ, our Passover, was sacrificed for us.” This is the night of Holy Thursday and Pascha.", see: ["JHN:1:29", "1CO:5:7"] },
    "EXO:14:21": { kind: "type", title: "The sea", body: "Through water, out of death, a people. Baptism is this crossing (1 Cor 10:1–2). Pharaoh’s host is the old tyrant drowned.", see: ["1CO:10:1", "ROM:6:4"] },
    "EXO:16:4": { kind: "type", title: "Manna", body: "Bread from heaven for the journey. John 6 will not let this stay in the wilderness: the true Bread is His flesh. The orarion and the diskos remember this daily.", see: ["JHN:6:32", "PSA:78:24"] },
    "EXO:17:6": { kind: "type", title: "The rock", body: "Water from the struck rock. “The Rock was Christ” (1 Cor 10:4). He is struck, and the side gives drink.", see: ["1CO:10:4", "JHN:19:34"] },
    "EXO:19:16": { kind: "type", title: "Sinai", body: "Cloud, fire, trumpet, a people trembling. Pentecost is Sinai given to all nations: not tablets of stone only, but hearts. The Feast of Weeks becomes the feast of the Spirit.", see: ["ACT:2:1", "2CO:3:3"] },
    "EXO:25:9": { kind: "type", title: "The tabernacle", body: "Made after the pattern shown on the mountain. Hebrews: the earthly holy place is a copy of the heavenly. Orthodox church architecture still walks this grammar — narthex, nave, sanctuary, veil.", see: ["HEB:8:5", "EXO:40:34"] },
    "EXO:26:31": { kind: "type", title: "The veil", body: "The veil hides the holy. At the Crucifixion it is torn from top to bottom. Hebrews: His flesh is the veil. Access is opened in the blood.", see: ["MAT:27:51", "HEB:10:20"] },
    "LEV:16:2": { kind: "type", title: "The Day of Atonement", body: "One day, one high priest, blood brought inside the veil, a goat for Azazel. This is the map of Holy Friday and of Hebrews 9. The difference: He enters once, having offered Himself.", see: ["HEB:9:7", "HEB:9:24"] },
    "LEV:17:11": { kind: "type", title: "The life is in the blood", body: "Blood is not magic; it is life offered. The chalice is this verse transfigured: we drink not to consume a thing but to receive a Person.", see: ["MAT:26:28", "JHN:6:53"] },
    "NUM:21:8": { kind: "type", title: "The bronze serpent", body: "Lifted up, that the bitten may look and live. Christ: “As Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, even so must the Son of Man be lifted up” (John 3:14).", see: ["JHN:3:14"] },
    "NUM:24:17": { kind: "type", title: "A Star out of Jacob", body: "Balaam’s unwilling prophecy. Nativity and Theophany take this star into the icons. The Magi already walk in this verse.", see: ["MAT:2:2"] },
    "DEU:6:4": { kind: "trinity", title: "Hear, O Israel", body: "The Lord is one. The Church does not hear a lonely monad. She hears the one essence of the three Persons. Christ names this as the first commandment, and then names Himself one with the Father.", see: ["MRK:12:29", "JHN:10:30"] },
    "DEU:18:15": { kind: "type", title: "A prophet like Moses", body: "Peter and Stephen name this as Christ (Acts 3:22; 7:37). The new Moses goes up not to receive the Law only but to become our Law from within.", see: ["ACT:3:22", "JHN:1:17"] },
    "JOS:2:18": { kind: "type", title: "The scarlet cord", body: "Rahab’s sign in the window is read as the Blood, and she herself — a Gentile, justified by hospitality and faith — stands in the Lord’s genealogy.", see: ["HEB:11:31", "MAT:1:5"] },
    "JOS:3:17": { kind: "type", title: "The Jordan", body: "A second Red Sea. The ark stands in the river-bed. Theophany will open this water again when the Lord steps in and the Spirit descends.", see: ["MAT:3:13"] },
    "JOS:5:14": { kind: "type", title: "The Captain of the Lord’s host", body: "Joshua falls on his face. The ground is holy. The Church has heard in this commander the Word, as at the bush.", see: ["EXO:3:5"] },
    "JOS:6:20": { kind: "type", title: "Jericho", body: "The city falls at the seventh circuit and the shout. Hebrews lists this among the acts of faith. The walls that fall are also the ones in us.", see: ["HEB:11:30"] },
    "RUT:3:9": { kind: "type", title: "Spread your wing", body: "The kinsman-redeemer covers the destitute. Boaz is a figure of Christ, who takes a Gentile bride and redeems the field of the dead husband.", see: ["MAT:1:5", "EPH:5:25"] },
    "1SA:2:1": { kind: "type", title: "Hannah’s song", body: "The barren sings; the mighty are cast down. Luke places this song on the Theotokos’s lips and makes it the Magnificat of the Incarnation.", see: ["LUK:1:46"] },
    "1SA:16:13": { kind: "type", title: "David anointed", body: "The Spirit rushes upon the youngest. Christos means Anointed. The shepherd-king from Bethlehem is already a sketch of the Shepherd from Bethlehem.", see: ["MAT:2:6", "LUK:3:22"] },
    "1SA:17:49": { kind: "type", title: "David and Goliath", body: "The young shepherd fells the giant with a stone. Holy Saturday and the hymns of Pascha know this as Christ felling Hades. The five stones have been read as the wounds, or as the senses made obedient.", see: ["HEB:2:14"] },
    "2SA:7:12": { kind: "type", title: "The promise to David", body: "A son, a house, a throne forever. This is the spine of the Gospel genealogies and of Gabriel’s word to Mary: “the Lord God will give Him the throne of His father David.”", see: ["LUK:1:32", "ACT:2:30"] },
    "2SA:12:13": { kind: "virtue", title: "I have sinned", body: "David does not negotiate. Psalm 50 (51) is born here. Repentance is the Orthodox first word, not the last resort.", see: ["PSA:51:1"] },
    "1KI:8:27": { kind: "type", title: "Will God indeed dwell", body: "Solomon’s astonishment is answered in the Incarnation: the One whom the heavens cannot contain dwells in a womb, a house of flesh, and now in the Church.", see: ["JHN:1:14", "COL:2:9"] },
    "1KI:17:14": { kind: "type", title: "The jar of flour", body: "Elijah and the widow: meal and oil that do not fail. The Fathers hear the widow’s faith and the unfailing table of the Lord.", see: ["LUK:4:25", "JHN:6:12"] },
    "1KI:19:12": { kind: "virtue", title: "A still small voice", body: "Not in wind, earthquake, or fire. The hesychast tradition takes this with great seriousness. Elijah must learn a different power before he crowns a king and a prophet.", see: ["MAT:17:3"] },
    "2KI:2:11": { kind: "type", title: "Elijah taken up", body: "A chariot of fire. Elijah stands at the Transfiguration because he did not see death as we do, and because the Forerunner comes in his spirit. The Ascension is the greater taking-up.", see: ["MAT:17:3", "ACT:1:9"] },
    "2KI:5:14": { kind: "type", title: "Naaman in the Jordan", body: "Seven dips, and the flesh of a Gentile becomes like a child’s. Theophany and baptism both live here. Elisha will not take payment: grace is not a fee.", see: ["LUK:4:27", "TIT:3:5"] },
    "PSA:2:7": { kind: "type", title: "You are My Son", body: "Royal psalm of David’s house, heard of Christ at the Jordan and the holy mountain (Heb 1:5; 5:5). The nations’ rage does not unseat this King.", see: ["MAT:3:17", "HEB:1:5"] },
    "PSA:22:1": { kind: "type", title: "My God, my God", body: "Greek Psalm 21. The crucified Lord takes this psalm on His lips. Read the whole: the pierced hands and feet, the lots for the garments, and then the great congregation’s praise. The Cross is not the last verse.", see: ["MAT:27:46", "JHN:19:24"] },
    "PSA:23:1": { kind: "type", title: "The Lord is my shepherd", body: "Greek Psalm 22. Christ names Himself the good shepherd (John 10). The table in the presence of enemies is eucharistic; the oil is chrism; the cup overflows.", see: ["JHN:10:11"] },
    "PSA:51:1": { kind: "virtue", title: "Have mercy on me, O God", body: "Greek Psalm 50, the daily psalm of the hours and of the Agpeya. David’s adultery becomes the Church’s voice. There is no Orthodox morning without this door.", see: ["2SA:12:13", "LUK:18:13"] },
    "PSA:110:1": { kind: "type", title: "Sit at My right hand", body: "Greek Psalm 109. The most-quoted psalm in the New Testament. David’s Lord is not David’s son only. Melchizedek’s priesthood is named here.", see: ["MAT:22:44", "HEB:1:13"] },
    "PSA:118:22": { kind: "type", title: "The stone the builders rejected", body: "Greek Psalm 117. Christ applies this to Himself. The verse of Pascha: “This is the day the Lord has made.”", see: ["MAT:21:42", "ACT:4:11"] },
    "PRO:8:22": { kind: "trinity", title: "Wisdom at the beginning", body: "The Fathers read Wisdom here as the Word, begotten not made, through whom the Father establishes the earth. Arian misuse of “created” is answered by the rest of the book and by John 1.", see: ["JHN:1:1", "COL:1:15"] },
    "PRO:9:1": { kind: "type", title: "Wisdom has built her house", body: "Seven pillars, a mixed bowl, a set table. The Church has heard the house as the Theotokos, and as the Church herself, and the meal as the Eucharist. “Come, eat of my bread.”", see: ["MAT:22:4", "JHN:6:51"] },
    "SNG:2:8": { kind: "type", title: "The voice of my Beloved", body: "He comes leaping on the mountains. Pascha and the Soul’s desire use this language without apology. The Bridegroom is Christ; the bride is the Church, and each soul in her.", see: ["JHN:3:29", "REV:19:7"] },
    "WIS:2:12": { kind: "type", title: "Let us lie in wait for the righteous", body: "They plot against the just man because he is inconvenient. The passion narrative is already audible. This is one reason the Church would not give this book up.", see: ["MAT:27:43", "ISA:53:3"] },
    "WIS:7:26": { kind: "trinity", title: "The brightness of the eternal light", body: "Wisdom as the unspotted mirror of God’s majesty. Hebrews 1:3 speaks this way of the Son: the brightness of His glory. The language is already Nicene.", see: ["HEB:1:3"] },
    "SIR:24:8": { kind: "type", title: "Let your dwelling be in Jacob", body: "Wisdom seeks a resting-place and pitches her tent in Zion. John 1:14 is this verse in flesh: the Word tabernacled among us. The Church also hears the Theotokos as that resting-place.", see: ["JHN:1:14"] },
    "ISA:6:3": { kind: "liturgy", title: "Holy, holy, holy", body: "The trisagion of the seraphim. Our liturgy still stands in this smoke. The coal that touches Isaiah’s mouth is read of the Eucharist, which burns and cleanses.", see: ["REV:4:8"] },
    "ISA:7:14": { kind: "type", title: "The virgin shall conceive", body: "Emmanuel, God with us. Matthew 1:23 takes this as the Nativity’s own caption. The sign is not a young woman in general; it is God in the flesh.", see: ["MAT:1:23", "LUK:1:31"] },
    "ISA:9:6": { kind: "type", title: "Unto us a Child is born", body: "Wonderful, Counselor, Mighty God, Everlasting Father, Prince of Peace. Nativity cannot be kept as a children’s pageant once this name is said.", see: ["LUK:2:11", "JHN:1:1"] },
    "ISA:11:1": { kind: "type", title: "A Rod from the stem of Jesse", body: "The Spirit rests upon Him: wisdom, understanding, counsel, might, knowledge, fear of the Lord. Chrismation names these gifts. The wolf and the lamb are the Kingdom’s peace.", see: ["MAT:3:16", "ROM:15:12"] },
    "ISA:53:5": { kind: "type", title: "He was wounded for our transgressions", body: "The Servant song is the Passion in the prophets. Philip begins at this Scripture with the Ethiopian (Acts 8). Holy Friday is Isaiah 53 sung in wounds.", see: ["ACT:8:32", "1PE:2:24"] },
    "ISA:61:1": { kind: "type", title: "The Spirit of the Lord is upon Me", body: "Christ reads this in Nazareth and sits down: “Today this Scripture is fulfilled.” The Church’s anointing for the poor, the captives, and the brokenhearted starts here.", see: ["LUK:4:18"] },
    "JER:31:31": { kind: "type", title: "A new covenant", body: "Not like the one they broke. Law written on the heart. Hebrews 8 quotes this as the charter of the Church’s worship.", see: ["HEB:8:8", "MAT:26:28"] },
    "EZK:1:5": { kind: "type", title: "Four living creatures", body: "Man, lion, ox, eagle — the Church’s icons of the four Evangelists. The glory is mobile: God is not stuck in a fallen city.", see: ["REV:4:6"] },
    "EZK:37:5": { kind: "type", title: "Dry bones", body: "Can these bones live? Pascha and the general resurrection both answer. The Spirit, the breath, the rattling — this is Ezekiel’s Pentecost.", see: ["JHN:5:28", "1CO:15:52"] },
    "EZK:44:2": { kind: "type", title: "The closed east gate", body: "The Lord has entered, and the gate shall be shut. The Church reads the ever-virginity of the Theotokos here, and the uniqueness of the Incarnation.", see: ["LUK:1:34"] },
    "EZK:47:1": { kind: "type", title: "Water from the temple", body: "A river from the right side of the house, deepening, healing the Dead Sea. Baptism, the pierced side, and the Spirit as living water all meet here.", see: ["JHN:7:38", "JHN:19:34"] },
    "DAN:3:25": { kind: "type", title: "A fourth in the fire", body: "Like a son of God. The three youths are a Paschal icon: the fire does not own them. The Song of the Three (in the Greek Daniel) is Matins of Holy Saturday.", see: ["DAG:3:1"] },
    "DAN:7:13": { kind: "type", title: "One like a Son of Man", body: "Coming with the clouds, given dominion. This is the Lord’s favorite title for Himself, and the confession at His trial.", see: ["MAT:26:64", "REV:1:13"] },
    "JON:1:17": { kind: "type", title: "Three days in the fish", body: "Christ: the sign of Jonah. The belly of the fish is Hades; the vomiting onto dry land is the Resurrection. Icons of Jonah belong at Pascha.", see: ["MAT:12:40"] },
    "MIC:5:2": { kind: "type", title: "Bethlehem Ephrathah", body: "Little among the thousands of Judah, yet from you the Ruler. The chief priests know this verse when Herod asks. Nativity is Micah’s.", see: ["MAT:2:6"] },
    "ZEC:9:9": { kind: "type", title: "Your King comes, humble", body: "A donkey’s colt. Palm Sunday is this verse staged in Jerusalem. The meek King is not a lesser king.", see: ["MAT:21:5"] },
    "ZEC:12:10": { kind: "type", title: "They shall look on Me whom they pierced", body: "John 19:37 names this at the lance-wound. Revelation 1:7 takes it to the coming. The wound remains in the risen body.", see: ["JHN:19:37", "REV:1:7"] },
    "MAL:4:2": { kind: "type", title: "The Sun of Righteousness", body: "Greek numbering places this in chapter 3. Healing in His wings. Nativity and Pascha both claim this dawn. The Forerunner is the next verse’s Elijah.", see: ["LUK:1:78", "MAT:11:14"] },
    "TOB:12:15": { kind: "liturgy", title: "I am Raphael", body: "One of the seven angels who stand in the presence. The Church’s naming of the archangels is not decoration; it is this verse living in the calendar.", see: ["REV:8:2"] },
    "WIS:3:1": { kind: "liturgy", title: "The souls of the righteous are in the hand of God", body: "A funeral and memorial verse in the Orthodox world. Suffering is not the last word over the just.", see: ["REV:6:9"] },
    "SIR:44:1": { kind: "liturgy", title: "Let us now praise famous men", body: "The ancestor of the synaxarion. The Church does not remember the dead as a civic honor roll but as a living choir.", see: ["HEB:11:1"] },
    "MAT:1:23": { kind: "type", title: "Emmanuel", body: "Isaiah’s sign, now a child in Joseph’s house. The whole Gospel is this Name walking around Galilee.", see: ["ISA:7:14"] },
    "MAT:2:15": { kind: "type", title: "Out of Egypt I called My Son", body: "Hosea’s line about Israel is taken of the Child. The true Israel is this Boy. Exodus happens again in Him.", see: ["HOS:11:1"] },
    "MAT:3:16": { kind: "trinity", title: "The Jordan", body: "The Son is baptized; the Spirit descends; the Father speaks. Theophany is the public Trinity. Our baptism is into this Name.", see: ["GEN:1:26", "MAT:28:19"] },
    "MAT:5:17": { kind: "type", title: "Not to destroy but to fulfill", body: "The Law and the Prophets are not a ladder we kick away. They are a face we now recognize.", see: ["LUK:24:27"] },
    "MAT:12:40": { kind: "type", title: "The sign of Jonah", body: "Three days and three nights. Holy Saturday is the Church keeping this sign without rushing the tomb.", see: ["JON:1:17"] },
    "MAT:17:2": { kind: "type", title: "He was transfigured", body: "Moses and Elijah — Law and Prophets — stand with the Word they wrote. The uncreated light is not a metaphor. Tabor interprets Sinai and Carmel.", see: ["EXO:34:29", "2PE:1:17"] },
    "MAT:26:26": { kind: "liturgy", title: "This is My body", body: "Passover becomes Eucharist. The type sits down at the table of its truth. “For the remission of sins” is Jeremiah’s new covenant in a cup.", see: ["EXO:12:8", "JER:31:31"] },
    "MAT:27:51": { kind: "type", title: "The veil torn", body: "From top to bottom: God’s act, not man’s. The way in is opened. Hebrews had already taught us what this means.", see: ["EXO:26:31", "HEB:10:19"] },
    "MAT:28:19": { kind: "trinity", title: "The Name", body: "Not names. One Name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit. Baptism is theology in water.", see: ["MAT:3:16"] },
    "MRK:15:39": { kind: "type", title: "Truly this Man was the Son of God", body: "A Gentile soldier says Mark’s title out loud, at the Cross, not at a miracle. That is this Gospel’s whole method.", see: ["MRK:1:1"] },
    "LUK:1:38": { kind: "type", title: "Let it be to me", body: "The New Eve’s obedience undoes the old Eve’s grasping. The whole Incarnation waits on this consent. The Church never tires of this verse.", see: ["GEN:3:6"] },
    "LUK:1:46": { kind: "type", title: "My soul magnifies", body: "Hannah’s song, now in a Virgin’s mouth. Vespers of the Orthodox world is full of this. God has regarded the humble.", see: ["1SA:2:1"] },
    "LUK:24:27": { kind: "type", title: "Moses and all the Prophets", body: "The risen Lord’s own hermeneutic. This app’s notes exist only as a thin echo of that walk to Emmaus. He is found in the breaking of the bread.", see: ["LUK:24:35"] },
    "JHN:1:14": { kind: "type", title: "The Word became flesh", body: "The center. Tabernacle language: He pitched His tent among us. We beheld His glory. All typology ends in this sentence.", see: ["SIR:24:8", "EXO:40:34"] },
    "JHN:1:29": { kind: "type", title: "The Lamb of God", body: "Exodus 12 walks into the Jordan valley. John the Forerunner names what the sacrifices were for.", see: ["EXO:12:3", "ISA:53:7"] },
    "JHN:2:9": { kind: "type", title: "Water become wine", body: "The first sign, at a wedding. The purification-jars of the old covenant are filled to the brim and become the feast. Cana is already the chalice.", see: ["EXO:7:20"] },
    "JHN:3:5": { kind: "type", title: "Born of water and the Spirit", body: "Baptism as new creation. Genesis 1’s water and Spirit over the deep are here personal.", see: ["GEN:1:2", "TIT:3:5"] },
    "JHN:3:14": { kind: "type", title: "As Moses lifted up the serpent", body: "Numbers 21, named. Looking at the crucified One is not sentiment; it is the appointed means of healing.", see: ["NUM:21:8"] },
    "JHN:6:51": { kind: "type", title: "The living bread", body: "Manna was the sketch. “The bread that I shall give is My flesh.” The scandal is the point.", see: ["EXO:16:4"] },
    "JHN:10:11": { kind: "type", title: "The good shepherd", body: "Psalm 22 (23) in the first person. He does not hire out the dying.", see: ["PSA:23:1", "EZK:34:23"] },
    "JHN:19:34": { kind: "type", title: "Blood and water", body: "The side of the new Adam. Baptism and Eucharist flow from the Passion. Eve was taken from a side; the Church is also.", see: ["GEN:2:21", "1JN:5:6"] },
    "ACT:2:17": { kind: "type", title: "I will pour out My Spirit", body: "Joel, now. Sinai for the nations. The Church is not an idea that caught on; she is a descent.", see: ["JOL:2:28"] },
    "ACT:8:32": { kind: "type", title: "He was led as a sheep", body: "Isaiah 53 is being read on the Gaza road, and Philip begins from that Scripture. This is how mission works: the type is named, and the Man is preached.", see: ["ISA:53:7"] },
    "ROM:5:14": { kind: "type", title: "Adam, a type of Him who was to come", body: "Paul says “type” out loud. The first man sketches the second, by contrast as much as likeness. Death through one; life through one.", see: ["GEN:3:6", "1CO:15:22"] },
    "ROM:6:4": { kind: "type", title: "Buried with Him through baptism", body: "The Red Sea, the Jordan, the tomb: one mystery. Walking in newness of life is not a metaphor bolted onto wet people.", see: ["EXO:14:21", "COL:2:12"] },
    "1CO:10:4": { kind: "type", title: "The Rock was Christ", body: "Paul teaches typology as apostolic fact, not as later decoration. The wilderness sacraments were already His.", see: ["EXO:17:6"] },
    "1CO:15:20": { kind: "type", title: "Firstfruits", body: "The sheaf waved in Leviticus becomes a Man out of the tomb. If He is not raised, the types were a cruel joke. He is raised.", see: ["LEV:23:10"] },
    "EPH:5:32": { kind: "type", title: "This is a great mystery", body: "Marriage, then Christ and the Church. Genesis 2 was already about more than a wedding in Eden.", see: ["GEN:2:24"] },
    "PHP:2:6": { kind: "trinity", title: "Being in the form of God", body: "The kenosis hymn. Equality with God is not grasped; it is poured out. This is the ethic because it is first the theology.", see: ["JHN:1:1"] },
    "COL:1:15": { kind: "trinity", title: "Image of the invisible God", body: "The firstborn of all creation — not the first creature, but the one through whom and for whom the creatures are. Arius stumbled here; Nicaea did not.", see: ["GEN:1:26", "HEB:1:3"] },
    "1TI:3:16": { kind: "trinity", title: "God was manifested in the flesh", body: "An early creed. The mystery of godliness is not a technique; it is the Incarnation confessed.", see: ["JHN:1:14"] },
    "TIT:3:5": { kind: "type", title: "The washing of regeneration", body: "Baptism as new genesis. The Spirit is poured out richly through Jesus Christ our Savior.", see: ["JHN:3:5"] },
    "HEB:7:1": { kind: "type", title: "This Melchizedek", body: "Genesis 14 and Psalm 109 (110) opened at length. A priesthood older than Levi, without beginning of days in the story’s telling, bread and wine — the Church’s liturgy is in this chapter.", see: ["GEN:14:18", "PSA:110:4"] },
    "HEB:9:12": { kind: "type", title: "Once for all", body: "Leviticus 16 completed. Not many times, not the blood of goats, but His own. The earthly copy gives way to the true holy place.", see: ["LEV:16:2"] },
    "HEB:10:1": { kind: "type", title: "A shadow of the good things", body: "The whole method of this app’s notes in one line. Shadow, not sham. The good things have come in Christ.", see: ["COL:2:17"] },
    "HEB:11:17": { kind: "type", title: "Abraham offered up Isaac", body: "The binding named as faith, and as a parable: God is able to raise from the dead. Moriah looks at the empty tomb.", see: ["GEN:22:2"] },
    "1PE:3:21": { kind: "type", title: "Baptism, the antitype", body: "The ark saved through water; baptism now saves — not as a bath of dirt, but as an appeal to God from a good conscience, through the Resurrection.", see: ["GEN:6:14"] },
    "2PE:1:4": { kind: "virtue", title: "Partakers of the divine nature", body: "Theosis, in apostolic words. Escape the corruption in the world through lust — the moral sense is not optional to the mystical one.", see: ["JHN:1:12"] },
    "REV:1:13": { kind: "type", title: "One like the Son of Man", body: "Daniel 7 stands in the midst of the lampstands. The Churches are not left to management theory; they are held in that right hand.", see: ["DAN:7:13"] },
    "REV:5:6": { kind: "type", title: "A Lamb as though it had been slain", body: "The center of heaven is not a general theism. It is Pascha, visible. Every Divine Liturgy stands here.", see: ["EXO:12:3", "JHN:1:29"] },
    "REV:12:1": { kind: "type", title: "A woman clothed with the sun", body: "Israel, the Church, and the Theotokos — the Fathers allow the overlap. The dragon waits; the Child is caught up. Nativity and the war in heaven are one canvas.", see: ["GEN:3:15", "LUK:1:31"] },
    "REV:21:2": { kind: "type", title: "The holy city", body: "The bride. Eden, Sinai, Zion, and the Church’s table all arrive. No temple, because the Lord God Almighty and the Lamb are its temple.", see: ["GEN:2:8", "EPH:5:32"] }
  }
};
